-A Laue lens consists of crystal rings arranged by radius. The orientation of the crystals in each ring is adjusted so that incident $\gamma$ rays from the target direction satisfy the Bragg condition and undergo Laue diffraction in transmission geometry. The diffracted ray is deflected from the incident direction by approximately $2\theta_{\rm B}$, so a crystal ring of radius $r$ focuses photons onto a small spot at focal length $F=r/\tan(2\theta_{\rm B})$, where $\theta_{\rm B}$ is the Bragg angle between the incident ray and the diffracting lattice plane \cite{Barriere2009Laue,Barriere2011LauePrototype}. For a fixed crystal thickness and mosaic spread, the ring effective area scales approximately as $f^2$ with focal length because both the ring radius and the available angular acceptance scale with $f$; reaching much larger collecting areas therefore requires proportionally longer focal lengths and more massive crystal assemblies, as in MAX-class Laue-lens concepts \cite{Weidenspointner2006MAX}.
-
-The design goal here is to obtain an effective area of order $20\,\mathrm{cm^2}$ within a balloon-compatible mass and focal-length envelope, using Ge(111), a standard mosaic spread of 30 arcsec, and a crystal thickness of order 1 cm. This target is physically consistent with a single-ring, $10\,\mathrm{m}$ focal-length configuration and does not require additional rings, nonstandard mosaic spreads, or kilogram-scale active crystal mass. The adopted configuration is f10m\_a1: a single Ge(111) ring with focal length $10\,\mathrm{m}$; a ring radius of about $74.28\,\mathrm{mm}$ inferred from the Bragg relation above; 25 square tiles of 18 mm side length; fixed tile thickness $10.2188\,\mathrm{mm}$; 30 arcsec mosaic FWHM; and active Ge mass $0.441\,\mathrm{kg}$. The tile thickness is tied to the valid range of the external rocking-curve calculation and is not varied with focal length.
-
-In the simulation chain, the Laue-optics branch is not only a standalone lens effective-area calculation. It defines both the focused-signal phase space and the boundary for background effects in the upstream optical mass. The focused signal is made of 511 keV photons that truly undergo Laue diffraction and cross the focal plane. Prompt atmospheric background and activation background produced in the upstream lens mass are treated as separate background streams and then propagated with the detector background through the same detector response, anticoincidence, and event selection. This keeps the focused signal, prompt background, and activation background on the same spectral-analysis footing, while preventing cosmic-ray-induced lens background from being mixed into the focal-plane focused-signal table.
-
-To generate the focused signal, the diffraction probability for 511 keV photons in each tile is not imposed as an external scalar efficiency. Instead, it is implemented in Geant4 as an application-level discrete process. This process assigns photons a finite effective diffraction mean free path and lets that process compete with standard Geant4 electromagnetic interactions, including photoelectric absorption and Compton scattering, in the same transport framework. Photons that are not selected for diffraction continue under the standard electromagnetic physics and may be absorbed, scattered, or transmitted. The diffraction probability is preferably interpolated from external XOP/CRYSTAL rocking-curve maps as a function of angular offset \cite{SanchezDelRio2011XOP}; when no external curve is available, the model falls back to an internal Darwin--Hamilton closed-form approximation. Mosaicity is represented by applying a Gaussian angular perturbation to the ideal Bragg-plane normal, with width set by the 30 arcsec mosaic FWHM. After diffraction, the outgoing direction is specularly reflected about the perturbed plane normal and the photon energy is unchanged. This separation between a transport-interface process and an independent probability/direction model follows the structure of existing Geant4 Bragg-reflection extensions \cite{Guan2023BraggReflection,Reiazi2025G4BraggReflection}; here the same process/model separation is applied to 511 keV astronomical Laue-lens signal generation rather than by porting that source code.
-
-Photons are emitted and transported tile by tile. When they cross the nominal focal plane, their energy, position, direction, and production history are recorded. Only tracked photons that have actually undergone Laue diffraction and fall within the detector Be entrance aperture are passed downstream; analytic spot projections are retained only as diagnostics and are not used as science injection sources because they ignore exit attenuation from the Ge crystal and would overestimate the focused flux. The effective-area normalization is the ring geometric area multiplied by the simulated fraction of diffracted photons that emerge through the Be aperture, giving $\aeff(511\keV)=20.1\,\mathrm{cm^2}$.
-
